\title[An exactly solvable isolated-horizon microstate model]
{An Exactly Solvable Isolated-Horizon Microstate Model:\\
the Bekenstein--Hawking Area Law, its Subleading Constant,\\
and its Hagedorn Thermodynamics}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete and exact analysis of a combinatorial microstate model of a
quantum isolated horizon in which a puncture carrying spin label $k=2j\ge 1$
contributes $k$ elementary quanta of horizon area and possesses $k+1$ internal
(magnetic) states. Writing $\Wc(A)$ for the number of ordered puncture
configurations of total area $A$, we prove that the infinite-order renewal
recursion $\Wc(A+1)=\sum_{i=0}^{A}(i+2)\Wc(A-i)$ collapses to the two-term linear
recursion $\Wc(A+2)=4\Wc(A+1)-2\Wc(A)$ for $A\ge1$, and hence that
$4\Wc(A)=(1+\sqrt2)(2+\sqrt2)^A+(1-\sqrt2)(2-\sqrt2)^A$. From this we deduce the
two-sided bound $(2+\sqrt2)^A/2\le \Wc(A)\le(2+\sqrt2)^A$; an area law
$\lvert\log \Wc(A)-A\log(2+\sqrt2)\rvert\le\log2$ with uniformly bounded defect;
the exact subleading behaviour $\log \Wc(A)-A\log(2+\sqrt2)\to\log\frac{1+\sqrt2}{4}$
at the exponential rate $(3-2\sqrt2)^A$, so that the unconstrained ensemble carries
no logarithmic correction; and uniqueness of the area quantum
$\gamma=4\log(2+\sqrt2)$ compatible with $S=A/4$. We then show that the area law is
structural: for an arbitrary degeneracy function with $\deg(1)\ge1$ and
$\deg(k)\le B^k$, supermultiplicativity and Fekete's lemma give a finite density
$L\in[\log\deg(1),\log 2B]$, and any positive root $r$ of $\sum_k\deg(k)r^k=1$
satisfies $L=-\log r$; the density is strictly monotone in the degeneracies,
positive except in one degenerate model, and computable with an exponential
truncation rate. Imposing the Gauss constraint $\sum_i m_i=0$ costs at most a
logarithm, $\Wc(A)^2\le(2A+1)Z(2A)$, with a parity superselection rule
$Z(\text{odd})=0$ that refutes global unimodality of the projection profile.
Finally, the canonical partition function converges exactly for $x<x_c=1/(2+\sqrt2)$
and equals $(1-x)^2/(2x^2-4x+1)$, yielding a Hagedorn temperature
$T_H=1/\log(2+\sqrt2)$; the first three area cumulants are rational with poles at
$x_c$ of orders exactly $1,2,3$ and residues exactly $x_c$, $x_c^2$, $2x_c^3$, the
variance (hence the specific heat) is strictly positive subcritically and diverges
at $x_c$, and the mean area diverges, establishing ensemble inequivalence.
\end{abstract}

